Let a,b be group bin counts, q raw rows per bin and m retained rows per bin. Write h_i for each retained bin mean embedding and K_ij=<h_i,h_j>. Author CTT computes

    T_native = (m/q)^2 ||sum_A h_i - sum_B h_i||^2/(ab),

whereas standard empirical-coreset biased MMD is

    T_MMD = ||sum_A h_i/a - sum_B h_i/b||^2.

The native Gaussian kernel sum is formed on selected coresets, but ctt.py divides by q². If a=b, T_native=(m/q)^2 T_MMD; this common positive factor cannot change a fixed configuration's permutation ranking. The native RFF code has analogous unequal-count signed-sum/(n1*n2) normalization without the compression factor. Tests used each method's exact native feature map for a mean-difference reference; goodpoints and Cheap ordinary RFF give the same diagnosed formula.

